# Shooting consistency experiments

OpMode: **Shooting | Consistency Experiments** (Diagnostics / TeleOp).

The test uses a fixed flywheel target of 1407 ticks/s, transfer target of 1440 ticks/s,
the previously validated gains, and intake power equivalent to 85% at 12.76 V.
Keep robot position, turret aim, launch geometry, and the centered ball start position fixed.
This OpMode commands no drivetrain or turret motion. It sets the intake ramp servo to UP,
matching the earlier Transfer Ball Validation setup. Check that this is the desired physical
shooting geometry before starting. Only one ball may be loaded per attempt.

## Current next step: contact/staging investigation

The OpMode now defaults to CONTACT_STUDY: 10 valid shots using the 300 ms ramp,
no boost, the 1407 ticks/s flywheel target, and the same automatic feed/recovery cycle.
Keep robot aim/distance fixed. Reuse the same marked ball if practical and put it at
the same centered intake starting spot for every attempt. Leave ball ID at 1 if you
reuse one ball. If you switch balls, use G2 D-pad UP/DOWN at LOAD to select a distinct
ID (1-99); IDs must consistently refer to the same physical balls for useful analysis.

At LOAD, G2 A confirms loaded. At RESULT: X=hit, Y=overshot, B=undershot,
D-pad LEFT=invalid/repeat. Complete 10 valid attempts, STOP and send the three
`shooting-consistency-contact_study-TIMESTAMP*` files from the usual folder.

The sample CSV now includes ball ID, elapsed feed time, measured loop interval,
raw encoder positions, and diagnostic 100 ms position-slope velocity. Position reads
have separate approximate midpoint timestamps; raw SDK velocity uses the loop timestamp.
CONTACT_STUDY writes a row every loop; this observes the existing loop rate rather than
guaranteeing a faster one. Position-slope values are delayed/smoothed and may be NaN
until enough observations exist; they never replace controller feedback.
Raw position/position-slope columns are collected only in CONTACT_STUDY.

The trial CSV also records an estimated first sustained slowdown onset, the mean
flywheel/transfer/intake speeds over the preceding 120 ms, the flywheel minimum
inside that slowdown window, transfer/intake speed and voltage at the minimum, the
integrated speed deficit, and recovery duration measured from the slowdown onset.
The previous recovery_s column still measures recovery after the 2 s feed ends.
Recovery from estimated contact requires speed at least baseline minus 25 ticks/s
for 120 ms. Deficit integrates positive speed loss below pre-feed baseline during
the estimated event; it is an approximate encoder-distance deficit in ticks, not energy.

Slowdown detection requires a drop greater than 35 ticks/s relative to the preceding
pre-feed mean for at least 40 ms. This does not prove ball contact, ball exit time,
or ball exit velocity. No detected event remains unknown (NaN), never a measured zero.
Diagnostic timing gaps are flagged and excluded from report slowdown averages. If a
slowdown never recovers before RESULT, its recovery estimate stays NaN. Existing
contact_events_estimated counts may differ because they use the original threshold
and can split fluctuations into multiple events. Missing/double-ball attempts still
need an INVALID mark even if a slowdown was detected.

The report groups estimated event timing/speeds by HIT, UNDER and OVER, allowing us
to look for associations with undershots. Ball ID, starting location, aim, and battery
still matter. A run of all hits or too few misses cannot establish what causes undershots.

## Focused boost comparison

Select BOOST_COMPARE before START. It runs 10 valid shots alternating no boost
and +0.6 V (five per setting), always with the 300 ms transfer ramp and a 1407 ticks/s
flywheel target. The voltage boost starts 100 ms before feeding and lasts through the
2 s feed. The selectable BOOST profile does not affect this routine.

Use the same LOAD / G2 A / RESULT controls below. Invalid attempts repeat the current
setting without advancing alternation. After COMPLETE, STOP and send all three
`shooting-consistency-boost_compare-TIMESTAMP*` files from the usual flywheel-tuning
directory. Hold position, aim, and centered ball placement constant across all shots.

## Fixed-profile validation

Select VALIDATE with G1 D-pad RIGHT before START. This routine runs 10 valid shots with the 300 ms
transfer ramp and no flywheel boost, at 1407 ticks/s. The BOOST profile selector does
not affect validation. Keep the same aim/distance as the comparison runs.

Press START, load one ball at each LOAD prompt, then press G2 A. Mark results only
after RESULT appears: G2 X=hit, Y=over, B=under, D-pad LEFT=invalid/repeat. Press STOP
after COMPLETE. Repeat the whole run with a different battery charge, without swapping
batteries during an active OpMode or deliberately discharging below normal team limits.
Send the three `shooting-consistency-validate-TIMESTAMP*` files from each run.
These records measure hit rate, not the geometric spread within the target.

## First run: transfer comparison

1. Build/install, select the OpMode, and press INIT. Select TRANSFER using G1 D-pad RIGHT.
2. Press START. All motor commands remain zero until you confirm a loaded ball.
3. In LOAD, place one ball at the intake entrance, clear of the flywheel throat.
4. Move hands clear and press G2 A once. This authorizes one automatic shot cycle.
5. Wait through SPINUP, optional BOOST, FEED, and RECOVER.
6. At RESULT, confirm exactly one ball fired and mark G2 X = hit, G2 Y = overshot,
   or G2 B = undershot. Use G2 D-pad LEFT for a sideways miss, no ball, multiple balls,
   uncertain result, or another invalid attempt. Invalid attempts repeat the same setting.
7. Repeat from LOAD until COMPLETE, then press STOP.

The 12 valid trials compare six interleaved baseline shots with three shots at each
transfer ramp duration (150 ms and 300 ms). Baseline transfer pre-spins before feeding;
ramp trials start transfer from zero as intake feeding begins. Final transfer velocity
is identical in all three profiles. Candidate order reverses in the middle repeat.

Readiness requires flywheel speed within +/-25 ticks/s for 200 ms; baseline also requires
transfer within +/-60 ticks/s. Intake feeding lasts 2 s. Flywheel recovery to its baseline
target must stabilize before RESULT; there is at least 2 s between feed completion and RESULT.
Waiting for loading or a shot mark has no timeout.

## Second run: boost comparison

Review the transfer results first. Before START, G1 D-pad RIGHT selects BOOST.
G1 D-pad UP cycles the transfer profile used for all boost trials:
PRESPIN, RAMP_150_MS, RAMP_300_MS. Use the profile selected from the first run.

Follow the same load/start/mark process. The 24 valid trials compare:

- Baseline: no boost (12 interleaved shots).
- Extra voltage: +0.3 V or +0.6 V, beginning 100 ms before feeding (three shots each).
- Higher speed target: +2% or +4%, beginning 150 ms before feeding (three shots each).

Boost lasts through the 2 s feed. The controller returns to the original speed target
during recovery. These are fixed experimental settings; no adaptive learning is active.
Readiness uses the original 1407 ticks/s target before the boost is applied.

## Faults and files

G1 B immediately aborts all three motor commands. Velocity/battery/timing faults,
wrong flywheel/transfer encoder direction, sustained stalls, readiness/recovery timeouts,
and unexpected motor modes also latch a fault. With the obstruction resolved and motors
clear, G1 X returns to LOAD; a fresh G2 A press is required to retry. Storage faults require
restarting the OpMode so no shots continue without usable logs.

The intake encoder is checked by absolute speed because its sign is not needed for
power control. Stall detection uses a conservative near-zero threshold, not a calibrated
ball-jam detector. Encoder dip counts are estimates and cannot establish that one ball fired.

Files are saved under `Android/data/org.firstinspires.ftc.teamcode/files/flywheel-tuning/`:

- `shooting-consistency-transfer-TIMESTAMP.csv` (or `boost`): sampled motor/target/phase data.
- Matching `-trials.csv`: landing marks, settings, speed range, RMS error, recovery and voltage.
- Matching `-report.txt`: valid/invalid counts, hit rate, and motor metrics per setting.

Results are flushed after each mark and samples at least every half-second. STOP closes
files and saves the current summary, including a fault if present. Send all three files.
Compare actual shot results alongside speed metrics: smaller speed dips alone do not
prove better placement. Three candidate repeats screen possibilities; repeat the best
setting against baseline before changing competition behavior.

## Controller changes

The shared PI controller retains its 0.4 V integral bound, integration error band, and
anti-windup. It now clears old integral trim for target steps greater than 50 ticks/s
and includes added shot voltage in saturation calculations. Tiny distance-target changes
retain the trim. No gain changes are applied by the experiment. Additional stall/readiness
and logging safeguards are local to the experimental OpMode.

Run desktop checks with `python3 tests/flywheel/run.py` and the cached-SDK compile check
with `python3 tests/flywheel/check_compile.py`.
